# The guest's memory, kept small so the host gets back what the guest is not using.
#
# Compressed swap: fstab.dsa declares a zram device of half the guest's memory. Idle apps'
# pages are kept there compressed (about a third of their size) instead of resident, and the
# memory they leave is free for page reporting to hand back to Hyper-V. Swapping to zram is
# cheap, so the kernel is told to prefer it over dropping file pages it would read again.
# Reading one swapped page reads only that page: zram has no seek to save by reading ahead, and
# the neighbours would be resident again for nothing.
#
# Free page reporting hands back only whole free blocks of its order (up to 2 MiB). Memory an
# app frees is scattered in small pieces among pages still in use, and until they gather into
# whole blocks none of it goes back. The kernel compacts in the background whenever free
# memory is more fragmented than this setting allows; 40, twice its default, has it gather
# free pages sooner, so more of them reach the host without waiting for the control daemon's
# reclaim.
#
# zram compresses with LZ4, fast enough for every swap. zstd, its second compressor, compresses
# what stays in zram again during the idle reclaim (dsa-channeld.rc), about a fifth smaller. It
# must be named before swapon_all sizes the device; a kernel without multi-compression refuses
# the write, and zram then keeps LZ4 alone.
on boot
    write /sys/devices/virtual/block/zram0/recomp_algorithm "algo=zstd priority=1"
    swapon_all /vendor/etc/fstab.dsa
    write /proc/sys/vm/swappiness 100
    write /proc/sys/vm/page-cluster 0
    write /proc/sys/vm/compaction_proactiveness 40
